{
  "_why": "Role track for MILLING, the second pack authored under docs/profiling/role-pack-authoring-guide.md and the first real test of whether adding a trade is a data change. fam_machining stays the generic machining interview bound at ISCO unit 7223; this pack binds the four NCO milling occupations directly (job_domain specificity 50 beats unit 40), so a miller gets milling depth and every other machining worker is untouched. The ratified BadaBhai sheet is a VMC sheet, so parity for this pack is measurable against a document rather than against a judgement.",
  "_scope": "VMC AND CONVENTIONAL MILLING, mirroring the turner's owner ruling of 2026-08-28 for the same reason: binding only the two CNC codes (7223.5004/5005) would make the pack reachable by 'vmc operator' and 'वीएमसी' alone, while every natural word for the trade -- milling machine, मिलिंग -- routes to 7223.1201 'Miller/Operator-Conventional Milling' and would fall to the six generic machining questions. Adding 7223.1201 and 7223.1400 takes reach from two phrases to four WITHOUT touching the alias corpus. The gate question therefore says 'Milling', not 'VMC': a conventional miller must be able to answer it honestly. The machine question offers 'Conventional milling machine', and controller / axis_capability both carry an escape chip, so nothing here forces a conventional miller to claim CNC skills. Pinned by packages/db/src/question-pack-reachability.test.ts.",
  "_deliberately_not_bound": "'cnc', 'cnc operator', 'cnc machine' and 'machinist' stay on fam_machining, and this pack does NOT take them. That routing is a deliberate ruling recorded in the reachability test: those words are said by turners and millers alike, and qp_machining's first question (machine_type) disambiguates. Claiming them for milling would ask a turner about fixtures and rotary tables -- the exact mirror of the argument that kept them off the turning pack.",
  "_depth": "THE TIER GATE IS milling_experience, and it is asked FIRST. It cannot be experience_years from qp_universal: phase order runs occupation_specific BEFORE universal_tail, so the universal answer does not exist yet when these items are selected. Its options carry value_number ONLY. pack-registry.service.ts resolves valueText ?? valueNumber ?? valueBool, so adding a value_text would make the answer a STRING; every gte below would then compare a string to a number, compare() returns null, and the predicate is false forever. A permanently-false ask_if is a question that is never asked, silently -- the #776 defect that sat in qp_welding for the life of the pack.",
  "_budget": "MAX_ENGINE_ASKS is 28 and qp_universal spends 8. Tier 0 is 8 items (16 total), tier 1 adds 3 (19), tier 2 adds 4 (23) -- the same shape as the turner, and for the same reason. The THREE fresher items are gated on milling_experience <= 0, which a senior worker never sees: a fresher skips tiers 1 and 2 entirely (7 items), so his worst case is 16 + 3 = 19, while the senior's 23 is unchanged. The tiers are mutually exclusive by construction, which is why this costs the senior nothing.",
  "_shared_attribute_names": "material_worked, measuring_tools, drawing_reading, setting_operation, programming_level, tolerance_band, sector_worked, workholding, quality_work and troubleshooting are attribute names this pack SHARES with qp_cnc_turning, and their option sets are deliberately NOT identical -- EN8 and EN31 are separate chips here because the ratified sheet prints them separately, and a snap gauge is a milling instrument where a plug/ring gauge is a turning one. Every dictionary that reads these names is keyed by pack_id (R12 §2), so the divergence is expressible. Before that change it would not have been: a milling worker's answers would have been read through the turner's vocabulary.",
  "pack_id": "qp_vmc_milling",
  "version": 1,
  "family_id": "fam_vmc_milling",
  "locale": "hi-IN",
